        "name": "compaction-transcript-rendering-with-byte-caps",
        "what": "A compacted segment is rendered into self-contained markdown by a pure, I/O-free function rather than being summarized into an opaque blob. The crate doc describes it as pure rendering of a compacted segment into self-contained markdown aligned with a Python reference implementation (`render_segment_to_markdown` / `compute_turn_stats`, with an index built incrementally through `INDEX_HEADER` + `render_index_row`), matching headers, sections, detail levels and INDEX columns but not bytes (xai-compaction-transcript/src/lib.rs:1-6). Segments are written as `segment_<n>` files under a `compaction` directory with an `INDEX.md`, and each segment is bounded at `SEGMENT_MAX_BYTES = 512 * 1024` with a recorded truncation notice (lib.rs:16-22). Detail levels are expressed as character budgets - `BALANCED_TEXT_CHARS = 2000`, `BALANCED_RESPONSE_CHARS = 500`, `LAST_RESPONSE_EXCERPT_CHARS = 500` - plus a flat `PER_TURN_OVERHEAD_BYTES = 64` (lib.rs:25-30).",
