          "xai-grok-tools"
        ],
        "verified": true
      },
      {
        "name": "bounded-tool-output-with-mcp-spill",
        "what": "Tool output is bounded in bytes at several layers, and an oversized MCP payload is spilled to disk with a pointer rather than dropped. The built-in bounds are `DEFAULT_TOOL_OUTPUT_BYTES = 40_000` and `DEFAULT_TOOL_OUTPUT_CHARS = 20_000` (xai-grok-tools/src/lib.rs:7, :11), with a `TruncationConfig` resolving per-tool over default and keeping a separate `mcp_max_output_bytes` so an MCP-specific override never changes non-MCP readers (xai-grok-tools/src/types/context.rs:16-61). `truncate_mcp_text` writes the full payload into the session's `mcp/` directory and appends a pointer plus a tool-specific steering hint (xai-grok-tools/src/util/mcp_truncate.rs:200-219). Two strategies are distinguished rather than conflated: `truncate_line` discards beyond the limit while `soft_wrap_line` preserves all content for tools where the whole output matters (xai-grok-tools/src/util/truncate.rs:12-21, :47-56), and `truncate_with_preview` always states the real total size even when the text on hand fits (truncate.rs:129-158).",
        "invariants": [
          "The spill filename is sanitized from the wire-supplied `call_id`, so a `/` or `..` cannot escape the session `mcp/` directory.",
          "All truncation is UTF-8-boundary-safe; `truncate_str` walks back at most 3 bytes.",
          "The MCP cap is resolved host-side once and published process-wide so free-function dispatch sees the same value as the config path.",
          "The reported total size is the real one, not the size of the retained excerpt."
        ],
        "failurePolicy": "fail-soft - content is degraded with an explicit footer rather than silently dropped, and a missing session folder simply omits the spill file.",
