      {
        "name": "process-group-terminal-kill-and-background-task-cap",
        "what": "Command execution is isolated behind a terminal-backend abstraction with local, streaming, ACP-remote and PTY implementations, and its timeout semantics are about process trees rather than processes. Timeouts are absolute deadlines raced inside a `tokio::select!` so a blocked read branch cannot starve the timer (xai-grok-shell-terminal/src/streaming_local_terminal.rs:517-519, :608) - the code notes that if a read blocks, the loop stalls and the command timeout cannot fire until the next iteration. On timeout the child's whole process group is killed so grandchildren cannot hold the output pipes open (xai-grok-shell-terminal/src/local_terminal.rs:114-120), and the reaped corpse is awaited under a separate short cap (streaming_local_terminal.rs:25, :222). Background tasks are capped per session and may be waited on with an optional timeout (background_task.rs:59, :151-178). The PTY session keeps a 256 KiB output ring buffer and batches notifications every 16 ms (pty_session.rs:52-53).",
        "invariants": [
          "Kill must target the process group, not the direct child - killing only the child leaves grandchildren holding the output pipes.",
          "A timeout must return the synthetic result promptly rather than waiting for the killed tree to be reaped.",
          "The timeout must be an absolute deadline selected against, because a blocking read otherwise defers it indefinitely.",
          "The PTY output buffer is a bounded ring; it must not grow with output volume."
        ],
        "failurePolicy": "mixed - a timeout returns a synthetic result marked `timed_out: true` with signal \"timeout\"; an unreapable uninterruptible child is abandoned to the runtime's orphan reaper rather than hanging the call.",
        "constants": [
          {
            "name": "DEFAULT_TIMEOUT",
            "value": "10 s",
            "evidence": "crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell-terminal/src/lib.rs:38"
          },
