      {
        "name": "mcp-multi-transport-with-in-process-acp-bridge",
        "what": "MCP is served over three transports, one of which avoids IPC entirely. The shipped set is an rmcp HTTP/SSE streamable client transport, a stdio child process transport with spawn-guard and process-group teardown, and a zero-IPC bridge typed as `AsyncRwTransport<RoleClient, DuplexStream, DuplexStream>` that tunnels each MCP JSON-RPC message to the SDK host as the ACP reverse request `x.ai/mcp/sdk_call` (xai-grok-mcp/src/acp_transport.rs:44; xai-grok-mcp/src/wire.rs:10-33). `start_mcp_server` builds one client per configured server, and the init state machine deliberately returns control to the session BEFORE per-server handshakes drain, tracking progress through a NotStarted/Starting/Finished state plus a handshaking set (xai-grok-mcp/src/servers.rs:248, :2577). A resilient transport wrapper decode-tolerates garbage stdio lines.",
        "invariants": [
          "reqwest 0.13 is a private implementation detail of the servers module and is deliberately NOT re-exported, because the rest of the workspace is pinned to reqwest 0.12 and a re-export would collide.",
          "The client event sender is private so callers must go through the setter, which fans the sender into already-owned clients; assigning the field directly would strand existing clients.",
          "The ACP bridge is declared HALF-DUPLEX: server-to-client traffic (notifications, sampling/createMessage, roots/list) is not bridged, and elicitation is not advertised on it."
        ],
        "failurePolicy": "mixed - a bridge reverse-call failure is fail-closed by design (a missing tool server is a real error), while a serve failure records an init-failed marker and surfaces the server as unavailable rather than aborting the session.",
