      {
        "name": "mcp-liveness-probe-and-token-fenced-retry",
        "what": "MCP clients are watched by polling rather than by a shutdown future, and retries are fenced by an attempt token. Each successful handshake arms a one-shot poller ticking every 500 ms calling `liveness_check()`; on the FIRST transport-closed observation while the client is Ready it emits exactly one transport-closed event and exits, and on a transient (state drifted out of Ready) it withdraws silently (xai-grok-mcp/src/liveness.rs:53, :121-180). The task clears its own liveness slot before exiting so a later arm can re-establish it, and client removal calls a cancel-watcher for the same reason. Separately, a spawn that fails for connectivity rather than auth records an unreachable marker and schedules a respawn after a cooldown; `take_unreachable_retry_candidates` moves a due server from Cooling to InFlight with a monotonic token, and the attempt must present that token to settle, so stale tokens no-op (xai-grok-mcp/src/servers.rs:856-896, :949). A separate attempt lease reclaims an InFlight entry whose future was cancelled mid-await and can therefore never settle.",
        "invariants": [
          "Polling is a consequence of the quarantined rmcp build: the running service exposes no shutdown future, so the only signal is a transport-closed predicate - replacing polling with a select would require patching rmcp itself.",
          "A watcher must never emit on a non-Ready state, because resetting the transport moves state out of Ready and a naive check would report a false transport-closed.",
          "The attempt token is the attempt's proof of ownership: no settle path may skip the check, or a cancelled attempt could install a client over a newer one.",
          "Config teardown must invalidate the token, or a removed server's retry could resurrect it.",
          "An in-flight server is never handed to another trigger, so retry triggers cannot dogpile the discovery and probe budget."
        ],
        "failurePolicy": "fail-soft throughout - a send failure during teardown logs at debug and exits without retry, non-Ready states exit silently, and an unreachable server degrades to unavailable with a scheduled retry rather than to permanent tool loss.",
        "constants": [
          {
            "name": "DEFAULT_POLL_INTERVAL",
            "value": "500 ms (MissedTickBehavior::Skip)",
            "evidence": "crates/codegen/xai-grok-mcp/src/liveness.rs:53, :135"
          },
          {
            "name": "UNREACHABLE_RETRY_COOLDOWN / ATTEMPT_LEASE",
            "value": "60 s / 600 s",
            "evidence": "crates/codegen/xai-grok-mcp/src/servers.rs:856, :860"
          },
          {
            "name": "ANONYMOUS_ACCESS_PROBE_TIMEOUT",
            "value": "3 s",
            "evidence": "crates/codegen/xai-grok-mcp/src/servers.rs:1134"
          }
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "crates/codegen/xai-grok-mcp/src/liveness.rs:53",
          "crates/codegen/xai-grok-mcp/src/liveness.rs:121-180",
          "crates/codegen/xai-grok-mcp/src/servers.rs:856-896",
          "crates/codegen/xai-grok-mcp/src/servers.rs:949"
        ],
        "modules": [
          "xai-grok-mcp",
          "xai-grok-shell"
        ],
        "verified": true
      },
      {
        "name": "mcp-oauth-consent-with-in-and-cross-process-dedup",
        "what": "HTTP MCP servers that advertise OAuth run a browser consent flow, deduplicated at two levels so a second trigger cannot open a second browser. In-process callers are deduped through a static in-flight map of server name to a watch receiver plus a generation counter; cross-process callers are deduped through an fs2 lock under the grok home, and a second process polls the credential store instead of re-authenticating (xai-grok-mcp/src/oauth.rs:15-24, :61, :224). Credentials live in `mcp_credentials.json` under the grok home behind an owner-only atomic-write store, and a disk-only classifier decides stored-credentials versus needs-login versus unknown WITHOUT a network round trip (xai-grok-mcp/src/auth_status.rs:42; xai-grok-mcp/src/credentials.rs:60).",
        "invariants": [
          "A stored entry with NO token response is an UNFINISHED login (needs-login), while an expired token that still has a refresh token classifies as stored - the distinction is what stops a half-completed consent from reading as success.",
          "On the disk path the observed token is seeded into the freshness record, so a re-authentication cannot read the same token back and treat it as fresh.",
          "The agent-id routing header is stripped from every caller-supplied HTTP/SSE config and re-added only by the spawn context - it is an advisory routing hint and never an authentication.",
          "Discovery must map a legacy-endpoint fallback onto 'no authorization support' rather than attempting a legacy flow."
        ],
        "failurePolicy": "mixed but scoped - a failure to construct the auth manager degrades to no-OAuth-support and falls through to the anonymous path (which is separately probed), while a hung authorization server is timeout-bounded rather than wedging the manager lock.",
