        "wiringNote": "Wired into the shell and enabled by default: the `SessionSearch` feature spec has `default_enabled: true` with env `GROK_SESSION_SEARCH` (crates/codegen/xai-grok-config-types/src/registry.rs:88-94). The index lives at `<grok home>/sessions/session_search.sqlite` under a per-host effective path on network mounts. A corrupt index self-heals by QUARANTINE rather than deletion: `heal_unusable` confirms corruption with `PRAGMA integrity_check`, renames the DB and its wal/shm/journal siblings to `<name>.corrupt.<unix_nanos>` under a `<db>.lock` exclusive lock, recreates empty, bumps a cache epoch, and retries the operation exactly once (src/recovery.rs:106-170). Sessions over 30 MiB get a title-only placeholder row so title search still works and an existing fuller row is never clobbered (src/bootstrap.rs:509-535)."
      },
      {
        "name": "tree-sitter-symbol-index-over-name-keyed-occurrence-map",
        "what": "The code navigation index is built from tree-sitter queries over five grammars (rust, ts/tsx, js, go, python) whose captures are classified by naming CONVENTION - `name.definition.*` and `name.reference.*`, plus `alias.original`/`alias.name` pairs - yielding `(name, Range)` occurrences (xai-codebase-graph/src/languages/mod.rs:36, :76). `IndexBuilder` enumerates through the git index (tracked files only) with an `ignore` walker as fallback, parses on a dedicated rayon pool using thread-local parser and query caches, keeps paths repository-root-relative so the cache is portable, and persists a hand-rolled little-endian format stamped with a query hash so a query change invalidates the cache (manager/builder.rs:100-101, :238; scope_graph/graph.rs:636-638).",
        "invariants": [
          "Capture names must keep exactly the three-part `name.definition.X` / `name.reference.X` form - the classifier matches three-element slices only.",
          "Every file must pass the size and binary filters BEFORE a parser is constructed.",
          "The index format version must be bumped with any layout change, because the reader hard-rejects other versions.",
          "The reverse file-to-definitions and file-to-references maps must be updated in the same call as the forward maps, or removing a file leaks entries."
        ],
        "failurePolicy": "mixed - build-level fail-closed (a thread-pool creation failure is an error), per-file fail-soft (unsupported extension, empty or oversized file, a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes, an unreadable file or a parse failure all simply leave that file absent), and manager fail-open (a builder error logs and yields an EMPTY index).",
